The first major non-farm employment data of 2026 came out with a bang, delivering a slap to everyone:

U.S. November non-farm job additions came in at just 50,000, directly shattering the market's expectation of 70,000, and marking a steep drop compared to the previous reading of 64,000;

The unemployment rate stood at 4.4%, slightly below the expected 4.5%, which seems relatively okay at first glance;
Even more alarming is that the combined non-farm data for October and November were revised downward by 76,000 — this isn't just 'moderate cooling,' it's solid proof that the labor market is truly weakening!

The past month has been utterly dull, especially in the U.S. Treasury market. The 10-year U.S. Treasury yield has been tightly stuck in a narrow range of 4.1% to 4.2%, with traders having no data, no policy moves, and no activity, forced to stare at the screen with nothing to do. Now that this data has come out, it has woken up everyone who was pretending to be asleep.

